{
  "about": "Golden CHECK forms. Each case is a CHECK as a developer writes it, the text SQL Server stores for it in sys.check_constraints.definition, and how the rule reader must read that stored text. tests/sqlserver_integration.rs builds the table in SchemaCraftCheckForms from this file and asserts every stored form, column list and enabled state against the engine; src/features/schema/rules reads every stored form against these columns and asserts the reading. Every expression is synthetic.",
  "engine": "SQL Server 2019 (15.x) and 2022 (16.x), default collation SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS",
  "notation": {
    "setup": "Batches run before the table is created.",
    "enabled": "false for a constraint disabled with NOCHECK; true when left out.",
    "columns": "The table columns the stored text references, in order of first appearance, each once. An unread case keeps them so they can still be highlighted.",
    "reading": "Either \"unread\" (the stored SQL is shown instead of sentences, and `why` says what is left out) or {rules, notes}.",
    "rules": "Sentences in order. A string is one sentence. {\"when\": [columns], \"rules\": [...]} holds sentences that apply only when every listed column has a value, because a CHECK accepts a row when its expression is UNKNOWN. {\"anyOf\": [...]} holds sentences of which at least one must hold. {\"whenEnabled\": [...]} holds the sentences of a disabled constraint.",
    "sentences": {
      "A > 0": "A compared with a literal, with `now` (the server's local time when the value is saved), with `utcNow` (the UTC time when the value is saved) or with another column. Operators: = <> < <= > >=. The column is always on the left; a literal written first is flipped. 'When the value is saved' and not 'when the row is saved': SQL Server checks a CHECK only when a statement writes one of its columns.",
      "between(A, 1, 5)": "An inclusive range.",
      "oneOf(A, 'x', 'y')": "One of the values, in the order they were written (the engine stores IN as an OR chain in reverse order).",
      "noneOf(A, 'x', 'y')": "None of the values, in the order they were written.",
      "required(A)": "A must have a value.",
      "notBlank(A)": "A must contain a character other than a space; trailing spaces are not compared.",
      "startsWith(A, 'x')": "LIKE 'x%' with no wildcard in x; endsWith and contains are LIKE '%x' and LIKE '%x%'. endsWith does not count trailing spaces of A, which a char or varchar value matched against a plain pattern may have; endsWithExactly is the same suffix when A or the pattern is Unicode (nvarchar, or varchar against N'...'), where they count. char and nchar pad a shorter value with spaces, which a Unicode suffix counts too, so on them a suffix is read only outside Unicode.",
      "matches(A, 'p')": "Any other LIKE pattern, explained by the pattern note. Left unread on char and nchar, where a _ can match a padding space, or a Unicode pattern fail on one.",
      "onlyDigits(A)": "NOT LIKE '%[^0-9]%' on varchar or nvarchar: a trailing space is refused like any other character, and the empty text is accepted. On char and nchar, which pad a shorter value with spaces, it is left unread.",
      "length(A)": "An operand: the number of characters of A, not counting trailing spaces (LEN).",
      "bytes(A)": "An operand: the size of A in bytes (DATALENGTH)."
    },
    "literals": "Numbers as the engine stores them, without the parentheses. Text in single quotes, with quotes doubled and without the N prefix.",
    "notes": "A set, in no particular order. pattern: the LIKE pattern needs explaining. collation: the rule compares text, so case, accents and ignorable characters follow the column's collation."
  },
